A||||3||Cooter||A suggestion for headlights||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||04-09-2000||08:54 PM||||It seems that in the past few days the number of topics about headlights has been at an all time high. <BR>If your Fiero's headlights do work, and you have not serviced the motors lately- then you really need to pull the motors apart and grease the bushings and inspect for damage caused by the bushings coming apart. 84-86's are a little bit of a pain and stand more of a chance of damaging the gears if the rubber bumpers have dried out and come apart. The 87-88's are simpler but could still benefit from a good cleaning and regreasing TODAY!!!! Like I said- If your's are working, do something to keep them going before they end up costing you money to fix.||||reg||1
Z||000001||sully5||04-09-2000||10:48 PM||||OK cooter, sounds like good advice. Is this very difficult? What kind of grease is recomended? Thanks for the info.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jhogans||04-10-2000||01:20 AM||||Thanks for the advice.  My headlights are making noise when they come up.  What grease should I use.  BTW, I have a 1986 Fiero GT.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-10-2000||09:08 AM||||to add a bit......<P>On the 87+ motors, while you have them apart, check the shear pins. If they even look funny just order the kit for both sides and replace them. No sense pulling them apart again later for 8$ in plastic pins.<P>As for grease..... something that stays reasonably stable in any temperature. This area gets pretty warm at times. (The motors themselves don't run long enough to generate significant heat.) <P>I haven't gotten to doing mine yet. So it's hard to say a specific type. Don't use something really heavy. Maybe some of that high temp silicone brake grease? It's not very heavy and very temperature stable. Allot of wheel bearing greases would be out.... they are really thick. If the grease is really thick it could actually bind when it gets cold.||||reg||1
